(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-anatomy-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"The oral cavity's superior boundary is formed by the:","choices":["Tongue and mylohyoid","Hard and soft palates","The teeth and lips","The oropharynx space"],"answer":1,"exp":"The oral cavity is bounded superiorly by the hard and soft palates, with the floor formed by the tongue and mylohyoid, the anterior/lateral walls by the teeth and lips, and it opens posteriorly into the oropharynx. — Slide 24 (Oral Cavity)"},{"q":"The space between the cheeks or lips and the gums is the:","choices":["Oral cavity proper","Vestibule","Vermilion border","Fauces"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vestibule is the slit-like space between the cheeks/lips and the gums and teeth, while the oral cavity proper lies internal to the teeth. — Slide 26 (Oral Cavity)"},{"q":"The greater omentum performs which notable function in the abdomen?","choices":["Forms the inguinal canal in the groin","Suspends the liver from the diaphragm","Anchors the kidneys to the posterior wall","Covers the transverse colon and small bowel"],"answer":3,"exp":"The greater omentum is a peritoneal fold hanging from the greater curvature of the stomach that drapes over and covers the transverse colon and loops of small bowel, and contains fat and immune tissue that can wall off infection. — Slide 19 (Greater Omentum)"},{"q":"The outermost layer of the esophagus is the:","choices":["Adventitia","Serosa layer","Mucosa layer","Submucosa"],"answer":0,"exp":"The esophagus is composed of mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis externa, with an outer adventitia rather than a serosa along most of its course, since it is not suspended within the peritoneal cavity. — Slides 42–43 (Histology of the Esophagus)"},{"q":"The inferior esophageal (cardiac) sphincter is described as:","choices":["Skeletal muscle under voluntary control","Circular smooth muscle that is not a true sphincter","A cartilaginous ring like the trachea","Completely absent in the human esophagus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inferior (cardiac) esophageal sphincter is a physiological sphincter made of circular smooth muscle rather than a distinct anatomical sphincter, and its competence is reinforced by the diaphragm's crural fibers. — Slide 46 (Esophagus)"},{"q":"Which component of the spleen supports hematopoietic function and blood filtration?","choices":["Hilum","White pulp","Splenic capsule","Red pulp"],"answer":3,"exp":"Red pulp is the site of blood filtration and (particularly in fetal life or disease states) hematopoiesis, removing old/damaged erythrocytes and recycling iron. — Slide 136 (The Spleen)"},{"q":"The jejunum occupies mostly which region of the abdomen?","choices":["Pelvis","Right lower quadrant","Right upper quadrant","Left upper quadrant"],"answer":3,"exp":"The jejunum, comprising the proximal ~40% of the small intestine distal to the duodenum, occupies mostly the left upper quadrant, while the ileum occupies more of the right lower quadrant. — Slides 75, 77 (Jejunum / Ileum)"},{"q":"The rectum forms approximately the last how much of the digestive tract?","choices":["15 cm","5 cm","30 cm","50 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rectum forms roughly the last 15 cm of the digestive tract, distal to the sigmoid colon, and functions to store feces prior to defecation. — Slide 101 (The Rectum)"},{"q":"The body of the pancreas is separated from the stomach by which peritoneal space?","choices":["The paracolic gutter along the colon","The greater sac anterior to the stomach","The rectouterine pouch near the rectum","The lesser sac (omental bursa)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lesser sac (omental bursa) lies posterior to the stomach and anterior to the pancreas, so the pancreatic body is separated from the stomach by this peritoneal space. — Slide 70 (Body & Tail of Pancreas)"},{"q":"Which liver lobe is bounded by the inferior vena cava and the ligamentum venosum?","choices":["Right lobe","Quadrate lobe","Caudate lobe","Left lobe"],"answer":2,"exp":"The caudate lobe is the posterior lobe bounded by the IVC on its right and the ligamentum venosum (fetal ductus venosus remnant) on its left. — Slide 115 (Lobes of the Liver)"},{"q":"The inferior mesenteric vein typically drains into which vessel of the portal system?","choices":["The splenic vein","The inferior vena cava","The hepatic vein","The renal vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior mesenteric vein most commonly drains into the splenic vein, which then joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the hepatic portal vein. — Slide 135 (Pancreas Veins / portal system)"},{"q":"The cystic artery, which supplies the gallbladder, most commonly arises from the:","choices":["Superior mesenteric artery","Left gastric artery","Splenic artery","Right hepatic artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cystic artery most commonly branches from the right hepatic artery, itself a branch of the proper hepatic artery, and typically travels through the cystohepatic triangle to reach the gallbladder. — Slide 142 (Common Hepatic Artery)"},{"q":"The inferior mesenteric artery supplies which region of the gut?","choices":["The midgut","The foregut","The hindgut","The stomach and spleen"],"answer":2,"exp":"The inferior mesenteric artery supplies hindgut derivatives, from the distal transverse colon through the upper rectum. — Slides 144–145 (Inferior Mesenteric Artery)"},{"q":"Which paired veins drain directly into the IVC?","choices":["Left gastric veins","Splenic veins","Superior mesenteric veins","Renal veins"],"answer":3,"exp":"The renal veins are paired vessels that drain directly into the IVC, alongside other paired tributaries such as the gonadal, suprarenal, phrenic, and lumbar veins. — Slide 146 (Inferior Vena Cava)"},{"q":"Lymphatic drainage grouped around the celiac artery would primarily drain which organs?","choices":["The lower limbs, draining via inguinal nodes","The rectum and anal canal, lower in the pelvis","The kidneys, via lateral aortic nodes","Foregut organs (stomach, liver, spleen, pancreas)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Celiac lymph nodes cluster around the celiac trunk and drain foregut derivatives — the stomach, liver, spleen, and pancreas — mirroring the celiac artery's vascular territory. — Slide 52 (lymphatic drainage)"},{"q":"Adipose tissue functions as a secondary endocrine tissue by secreting leptin and:","choices":["Calcitonin","Insulin","Resistin","Aldosterone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Adipose tissue secretes leptin (which signals satiety) and resistin (linked to insulin resistance), functioning as a secondary endocrine tissue beyond its role in fat storage. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 — Adipose Tissue)"},{"q":"The main endocrine organs include the thyroid, parathyroid, pituitary, pineal, and:","choices":["Sebaceous glands","Submandibular glands","Apocrine sweat glands","Suprarenal glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"The major endocrine organs include the pituitary, hypothalamus, thyroid, thymus, suprarenal (adrenal) glands, pineal, parathyroid, pancreas, and gonads. — Slide 3 (Overview)"},{"q":"The pancreatic islets are also known as the:","choices":["Islets of Langerhans","Acini","Follicles of Wirsung","Islets of Santorini"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pancreatic islets, the endocrine portion of the pancreas that secretes insulin and glucagon, are also called the islets of Langerhans. — Slide 52 (Pancreas histology)"},{"q":"The parathyroid gland is described histologically as being formed of:","choices":["Radial columns of lipid-laden cells within the adrenal cortex","Follicles lined by cuboidal cells and filled with colloid","Solid cell cords, well vascularized, with a capsule","Nests of chromaffin cells within the adrenal medulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"The parathyroid gland has its own capsule, is richly vascularized, and consists of solid cell cords composed of chief cells and oxyphil cells rather than follicles. — Slide 46 (Histology of parathyroid glands)"},{"q":"The right suprarenal gland's anterior relations include the liver and which vessel?","choices":["Hepatic portal vein","Abdominal aorta","Inferior vena cava","Left gonadal vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right adrenal gland is related anteriorly to the liver and the inferior vena cava, while the left adrenal gland relates anteriorly to the stomach and pancreas. — Slide 86 (Suprarenal Glands)"},{"q":"The adrenal glands receive arterial supply from the inferior phrenic artery, the renal artery, and the:","choices":["Middle suprarenal artery from the aorta","Splenic artery from the celiac trunk","Superior mesenteric artery branch","Testicular (gonadal) artery from the aorta"],"answer":0,"exp":"The adrenal glands classically receive a triple arterial supply: the superior suprarenal artery (from the inferior phrenic), the middle suprarenal artery (directly from the aorta), and the inferior suprarenal artery (from the renal artery). — Slides 81, 86 (Suprarenal Glands)"},{"q":"The gland whose chief cells are the principal PTH source lies in which location?","choices":["On the posterior surface of the thyroid","Within the sella turcica of the sphenoid","On the superior pole of the kidney","In the anterior mediastinum near the thymus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The parathyroid glands, whose chief cells are the principal source of parathyroid hormone (PTH), lie embedded on the posterior surface of the thyroid gland, typically as four small glands. — Slides 43–44 (Parathyroid Glands)"},{"q":"The gonads of the reproductive system are defined as the organs that:","choices":["Store and mature developing sperm","Produce gametes and hormones","Secrete seminal fluid components","Form the external genitalia only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gonads (testes in males, ovaries in females) are defined by their dual function of producing gametes (sperm/oocytes) and sex hormones (testosterone/estrogen and progesterone). — Slide 4 (Organization of the Reproductive System)"},{"q":"The tunica vaginalis, which lines the scrotal cavity, is derived embryologically from the:","choices":["Peritoneum","Tunica albuginea","Dartos fascia","Mesonephric duct"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tunica vaginalis is a peritoneal-derived serous membrane that is carried down into the scrotum along with the descending testis via the processus vaginalis. — Slides 13, 16 (tunica vaginalis)"},{"q":"The muscular contractions that expel semen during ejaculation are described as:","choices":["Sustained contraction of the bladder detrusor muscle","Coordinated peristaltic waves along the entire ureter","Powerful contractions of ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus","Simple relaxation of the external urethral sphincter alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ejaculation is produced by powerful, rhythmic contractions of the ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus muscles, which compress the base of the erectile bodies and propel semen through the urethra. — Slide 67 (Semen release and ejaculation)"},{"q":"The scrotal septum divides the scrotum into how many chambers?","choices":["Three","One","Two","Four"],"answer":2,"exp":"The scrotal septum, a midline extension of the dartos fascia, divides the scrotum into two separate chambers, one for each testis. — Slide 16 (Scrotum)"},{"q":"The two arteries traveling within the spermatic cord are the testicular artery and the:","choices":["Deferential artery","Internal pudendal artery","External pudendal artery","Inferior epigastric artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The spermatic cord carries the testicular artery (from the aorta) alongside the deferential artery (supplying the ductus deferens, typically from the superior or inferior vesical artery). — Slides 14–15 (Spermatic Cords)"},{"q":"Which of the following nerves supplies sensory and motor innervation to the scrotum?","choices":["Ilioinguinal nerve","Obturator nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Sciatic nerve branch"],"answer":0,"exp":"The scrotum is supplied by the ilioinguinal nerves, along with the hypogastric plexus, genitofemoral nerves, and pudendal nerves. — Slide 22 (The Scrotum and the Position of the Testes)"},{"q":"The acrosome of a sperm head contains which fertilization-critical enzyme?","choices":["Hyaluronidase","Chymotrypsin","Cholinesterase","Acid phosphatase"],"answer":0,"exp":"The acrosome, a cap-like vesicle over the sperm head, contains hydrolytic enzymes including hyaluronidase, which help the sperm penetrate the corona radiata and zona pellucida of the oocyte during fertilization. — Slide 38 (Anatomy of a Sperm)"},{"q":"Capacitation, which renders sperm capable of fertilization, is completed:","choices":["In the epididymis during storage","In the female reproductive tract","In the seminiferous tubules directly","In the prostate gland secretions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sperm gain initial motility from seminal secretions but only complete capacitation — the final biochemical changes needed to fertilize an oocyte — within the female reproductive tract. — Slide 45 (capacitation)"},{"q":"The male urethra is approximately how long overall?","choices":["8–10 cm","3–4 cm","18–20 cm","23–24 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The male urethra is approximately 18–20 cm long, spanning prostatic, membranous, and penile (spongy) regions, compared to the much shorter ~3–4 cm female urethra. — Slide 58 (Urethra)"},{"q":"The cervix, along with the uterus, is derived from which embryonic source?","choices":["The urogenital sinus epithelium","The fused caudal Müllerian ducts","The paired mesonephric ducts","The gubernaculum testis cord"],"answer":1,"exp":"The uterus and cervix (along with the upper vagina) develop from the fused caudal portions of the paired Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The Müllerian ducts arise from the coelomic epithelium on the lateral aspect of the:","choices":["Pronephros","Metanephros","Mesonephros","Allantois"],"answer":2,"exp":"The paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts arise from an invagination of coelomic epithelium on the lateral aspect of the mesonephros, the transient embryonic kidney. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"Which boundary landmark of the female perineum is located posteriorly, palpable at the superior end of the intergluteal cleft?","choices":["The pubic symphysis","The tip of the coccyx","The mons pubis","The ischial tuberosity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tip of the coccyx marks the posterior boundary of the perineum's diamond shape, palpable at the superior end of the intergluteal (natal) cleft. — Slide 8 (Boundaries of Female Perineum)"},{"q":"In the female, the anterior (urogenital) triangle contains the vaginal orifice and the:","choices":["Rectum","Anus","External os of the cervix","Urethral orifice"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior (urogenital) triangle of the female perineum contains both the urethral orifice and the vaginal orifice. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The cardinal (transverse cervical) ligaments run from the lateral cervix and vagina to the:","choices":["Sacrum","Pelvic wall","Labia majora","Pubic symphysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cardinal (transverse cervical) ligaments extend laterally from the cervix and upper vagina to the lateral pelvic wall, providing the primary support for the cervix and carrying the uterine vessels. — Slide 36 (Support of the Uterus)"},{"q":"The urethrovaginal sphincter is one of the muscles related to the vagina, alongside the external urethral sphincter and the:","choices":["Coccygeus muscle of the pelvic floor","Pubovaginalis (part of levator ani)","Iliococcygeus portion of levator ani","Obturator externus muscle laterally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Muscles related to the vagina include the pubovaginalis (a subdivision of the levator ani), the external urethral sphincter, the urethrovaginal sphincter, and the bulbospongiosus. — Slide 62 (Muscles Related to the Vagina)"},{"q":"The endometrium's blood supply is derived from the uterine, vaginal, and which arteries?","choices":["Ovarian arteries","Renal arteries","Femoral arteries","Pudendal arteries only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterine layers, including the endometrium, are supplied by the uterine, vaginal, and ovarian arteries, which anastomose to provide a rich blood supply. — Slide 35 (Uterine Layers)"},{"q":"Superior lymphatic drainage of the vagina goes to the internal and which other iliac nodes?","choices":["Superficial inguinal nodes","Common iliac nodes only","External iliac nodes","Para-aortic nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior part of the vagina drains to both the internal and external iliac nodes, while the inferior part drains more to the superficial inguinal nodes. — Slide 64 (Lymphatic Drainage of Vagina)"},{"q":"The finger-like projections at the distal end of the uterine tube are the:","choices":["Fornices","Fimbriae","Rugae","Papillae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fimbriae are the finger-like projections fringing the infundibulum, the distal, funnel-shaped end of the uterine tube, and help sweep the released oocyte into the tube. — Slide 51 (Uterine Tube)"},{"q":"The ovarian follicle consists of an oocyte surrounded by which cells?","choices":["Chief cells","Podocytes","Follicular cells","Chromaffin cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"An ovarian follicle is composed of an oocyte surrounded by a layer (or layers) of follicular (granulosa) cells, which support oocyte development and produce estrogen. — Slide 25 (Ovaries)"},{"q":"The broad ligament is continuous with which layer of the uterine wall?","choices":["The myometrium","The perimetrium","The endometrium","The mucosa"],"answer":1,"exp":"The broad ligament, a peritoneal fold draping over the uterus, uterine tubes, and ovaries, is continuous with the perimetrium — the outer serosal layer of the uterine wall. — Slide 39 (Wall of the Uterus)"},{"q":"In the female, the external urethral orifice is located:","choices":["Anterior to the vaginal opening, posterior to the clitoris","Located within the vaginal canal itself, not externally","Posterior to the anus, within the anal triangle","At the tip of the clitoris, anterior to the labia"],"answer":0,"exp":"In females, the external urethral orifice lies anterior to the vaginal opening and posterior to the clitoris, an important landmark within the urogenital triangle. — Slide 63 (Innervation / orifice)"},{"q":"The membranous urethra in the male is the segment that:","choices":["Extends from the prostatic urethra to the penile urethra, piercing the urogenital diaphragm","Passes through the substance of the prostate gland, proximal to the membranous segment","Runs the entire length of the penile shaft, coursing within the corpus spongiosum","Lies entirely within the muscular wall of the urinary bladder, proximal to the urethra"],"answer":0,"exp":"The membranous urethra is the short segment extending from the end of the prostatic urethra to the beginning of the penile (spongy) urethra, piercing the urogenital diaphragm (external urethral sphincter) along the way. — Slide 59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"Minor calyces converge to form which larger structure?","choices":["Renal papillae","Major calyces","Renal columns","Collecting ducts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Minor calyces receive urine draining from the renal papillae and converge to form the larger major calyces, which in turn join to form the renal pelvis. — Slides 22, 42 (Urinary Flow)"},{"q":"The perinephric fat serves primarily which function?","choices":["Endocrine hormone production","Filtration of the blood","Storage of urine output","Cushioning and insulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Perinephric (perirenal) fat completely surrounds the kidney between the fibrous capsule and renal fascia, primarily providing cushioning and insulation to protect and stabilize the organ. — Slide 13 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"The renal artery arises directly from which vessel?","choices":["Abdominal aorta","Common iliac artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Inferior vena cava"],"answer":0,"exp":"The renal artery arises directly off the abdominal aorta. — Kidney Location — Renal Artery off abdominal aorta"},{"q":"The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle leads directly into the:","choices":["Renal papilla","Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Distal convoluted tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The thick ascending limb returns toward the cortex and leads directly into the distal convoluted tubule. — Slide 39 (Loop of Henle)"},{"q":"In males, which structure lies posterosuperior to the urinary bladder?","choices":["The uterus","The prostate","The rectum","The vagina"],"answer":2,"exp":"In males, the rectum lies posterosuperior to the bladder, while the prostate lies inferior to the bladder neck. — Slide 51 (Urinary Bladder)"},{"q":"The EPO-producing, oxygen-sensing cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus secrete:","choices":["Mineralocorticoid","Renin enzyme","Thyrocalcitonin","Erythropoietin"],"answer":3,"exp":"The oxygen-sensing (REPOS) cells of the kidney sense low oxygenation and secrete erythropoietin, stimulating red blood cell production in bone marrow. — Slide 36 (Juxtaglomerular Apparatus)"}]};
